=====Usage notes=====
{{rfref|en}}
{{m-lite|en|me|Me}} is traditionally described as the [[objective]] pronoun, meaning it is used as the [[object]] of verbs and prepositions, while the [[subjective]] pronoun {{m-lite|en|I}} should be used as the [[subject]] of verbs. However, "objective" pronouns are widely used as the subject of verbs in colloquial speech when they are accompanied by a conjunction, for example, ''"me and her are friends"''. This usage is traditionally considered incorrect, and ''"she and I are friends"'' is the prescriptive construction.

Using {{m-lite|en|me}} as the lone subject of a verb (without a conjunction, e.g. ''"me want"'', ''"me like"'') is a feature of various types of both [[pidgin]] English and that of [[infant]] English-[[learners]], and is sometimes used by speakers of standard English for [[jocular]] effect (e.g. ''"me likee"'', ''"me wantee"'').

Although in some dialects 'me' is also used as a [[possessive]], in writing, speakers of these dialects usually write {{m-lite|en|my}}.

Some prescriptivists object to the use of {{m-lite|en|me}} following the verb {{m-lite|en|be}}, as in ''"It wasn't me"''. The phrase ''"It was not I"'' is prescribed as correct, though this may be seen as extreme and used for jocular effect.

