* As a clitic pronoun used before another clitic, it is pronounced unstressed and without {{w|syntactic gemination}} of the following consonant, e.g. {{m-lite|it|me}} {{m-lite|it|ne}} {{m-lite|it|vado||I'm going away}} {{IPAchar|/me ne ˈvado/}}. As a disjunctive pronoun used after a preposition, it is pronounced stressed and with syntactic gemination, e.g. {{m-lite|it|a}} {{m-lite|it|me}} {{m-lite|it|piace||'''I''' like him/her/it}} {{IPAchar|/a‿mˌme‿pˈpjatʃe/}} (since {{m-lite|it|a}} also triggers syntactic gemination).

